WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.360
Denmark paired generous benefits with mandatory training to protect workers, yet employers

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:12.120
cycled them through short contracts instead. This is unintended consequences, episode 128.

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:18.760
Danish policy makers in the 1990s faced high structural unemployment. They wanted to preserve

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:23.480
the country's tradition of flexible labor markets without exposing workers to the harsh

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:29.080
insecurity seen elsewhere in Europe. They expanded unemployment benefits to replace a large

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:34.760
share of lost income. They paired those benefits with active labor market programs that required

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:40.880
participation in training or job search activities. The logic was straightforward. Generous

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:46.120
support would let people take risks and change jobs. Mandatory training would keep their skills

00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:52.120
current and speed re-employment. At the time, this flat security approach looked like a pragmatic

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:58.280
middle path between rigid job protections and pure market flexibility. Decision makers believe

00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:03.440
the combination would maintain high overall employment while still shielding individuals from

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:08.980
long spells of poverty. The policy rested on the reasonable assumption that firms would continue

00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:14.460
to invest in workers once they were back inside the door. The training requirement would lower the

00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:19.920
cost of bringing someone up to speed. The benefit system would reduce the political pressure for stricter

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:26.320
firing rules. The model was rolled out through successive labor market reforms. Those reforms increased

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:30.860
increased benefit generosity while tightening activation requirements and expanding the network

00:01:30.860 --> 00:01:31.940
of training providers.

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:37.780
Early data showed quick drops in registered unemployment and high rates of program completion.

00:01:38.620 --> 00:01:41.520
Supporters cited those numbers as proof the system worked.

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:46.780
Proponents pointed to Denmark's continued high employment to population ratio, compared

00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:48.780
with many continental European peers.

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:53.780
A few labor economists noted that short-term contracts were becoming more common.

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:58.900
The dominant view was that any such pattern was a transitional cost on the way to better

00:01:58.900 --> 00:01:59.280
matching.

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:04.020
The policy framework remained largely intact through the early 2000s.

00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:09.120
Proponents argued that the visible success in re-employment statistics justified keeping

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:13.200
the activation thresholds high and the benefit replacement rates generous.

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:19.720
In a Danish manufacturing plant in the mid-2000s, a mid-skilled machinist finished one nine-month

00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:25.080
training course only to be let go and rehired three months later for another subsidized program

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:30.380
at a different firm. Employers discovered they could hire workers on temporary contracts,

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:35.800
receive subsidies for sending them to training, and then release them before the costs of firm

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:41.520
specific investment became significant. Because benefits remained available and training slots were

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:47.220
guaranteed, the financial risk of repeated short spells fell largely on the public system rather

00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:52.820
than on the firm. Mid-skill workers therefore moved through sequences of brief jobs and

00:02:52.820 --> 00:02:57.740
mandated courses without ever building the deep process knowledge or internal networks

00:02:57.740 --> 00:03:04.280
that drive wage growth inside a single organization. Over time, wage growth for this cohort lagged

00:03:04.280 --> 00:03:10.060
behind comparable workers in countries with less emphasis on rapid reentry and more emphasis on

00:03:10.060 --> 00:03:15.740
longer job tenure. The second order effect was a thinner accumulation of human capital across

00:03:15.740 --> 00:03:22.160
the mid-skill segment of the workforce. A third-order effect appeared in firm behavior. Some companies

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:26.940
reduced their own internal training budgets, knowing the state would supply the next round of

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:32.340
certified skills. The result was a labor market that looked flexible and secure on aggregate

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:37.940
statistics while quietly eroding the very skill-based the training requirement had been meant to strengthen.

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:43.880
If a firm saves the expense of six months of on-the-job mentoring by cycling a worker through an

00:03:43.880 --> 00:03:46.760
external course, and then lets the contract lapse.

00:03:47.320 --> 00:03:51.180
The public purse absorbs both the benefit payments and the course cost.

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:55.560
Workers facing this pattern had little leverage to demand internal advancement.

00:03:56.020 --> 00:04:00.300
The next training slot was always available, and the benefit bridge was reliable.

00:04:00.920 --> 00:04:06.640
Over repeated cycles, the absence of firm specific expertise showed up in slower productivity gains

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:11.900
and flatter earnings trajectories for precisely the mid-skill group the policy had aimed to protect.

00:04:11.900 --> 00:04:15.560
In fact, Danish authorities responded with incremental adjustments.

00:04:15.980 --> 00:04:21.300
They tightened rules on repeated use of temporary contracts and shifted some training resources

00:04:21.300 --> 00:04:23.860
toward longer, more specialized programs.

00:04:24.580 --> 00:04:30.160
Evaluations later suggested these tweaks reduced the most obvious cycling but did not fully

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:32.220
restore firm-level skill investment.

00:04:32.840 --> 00:04:36.640
The core architecture of benefits plus activation remained in place.

00:04:37.420 --> 00:04:40.000
Denmark continued to post strong employment figures.

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:49.020
Some analysts now argue that the model's success depends on complementary institutions such as strong collective bargaining that were already weakening.

00:04:49.620 --> 00:04:55.420
Even after the rule changes, firms retained the option to use short contracts for marginal roles.

00:04:55.900 --> 00:05:04.360
The incentive to shift training costs outward, persisted, wherever the public system continued to certify skills at low direct cost to the employer.

00:05:04.360 --> 00:05:10.220
Incentive structures always find their loopholes, when subsidies and requirements are attached

00:05:10.220 --> 00:05:15.280
to the act of training, rather than to the quality of the resulting match, rational

00:05:15.280 --> 00:05:17.220
actors optimize for the subsidy.

00:05:17.900 --> 00:05:23.600
Complex systems resist simple interventions because each new rule alters the return on investment

00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:27.780
for every participant, often in ways the designers did not model.

00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:33.780
Before layering benefits and mandates, it is worth asking how the policy changes the long-term

00:05:33.780 --> 00:05:37.040
calculus of skill investment for both sides of the labor market.

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:42.560
How might today's proposals for portable benefits and required upskilling alter the willingness

00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:44.480
of firms to train workers themselves?

00:05:45.380 --> 00:05:46.620
We'll leave it there for today.

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:48.960
Thanks for thinking through this one with me.

00:05:49.420 --> 00:05:54.080
If you know someone who loves a good cautionary tale, send it their way, and I'll see you tomorrow

00:05:54.080 --> 00:05:54.620
with another.

